==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*speḱ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|spectacle}}, from {{der|en|frm|spectacle}}, from {{der|en|la|spectāculum||a show, spectacle}}, from {{m|la|spectō||to see, behold}}, frequentative of {{m|la|speciō||to see}}. See {{m|en|species}}. {{doublet|en|spectaculum}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈspɛktəkl̩/}}
* {{hyph|en|spec|ta|cle}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Mélange a trois-spectacle.wav|a=UK}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|countable}} An exciting or extraordinary scene, exhibition, performance etc.
#: {{syn|en|pageant|show}}
#: {{ux|en|The horse race was a thrilling '''spectacle'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=But scarcely was it set upon the table when the whole was carried off and plundered by the people who came to see the '''spectacle''' of the entertainment|author={{w|Robert Southey}}|page=555|url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Choice_passages/RsE_AQAAMAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=choiceness&pg=PA555&printsec=frontcover|title=Choice passages|publisher=Reaves and Turner|year=1876|editor=John Wood Warter}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-1|act=I|scene=vi|page=100|column=1|passage=VVith ſcoffes and ſcornes, and contumelious taunts, / In open Market-place produc't they me, / To be a publique '''ſpectacle''' to all: / Here, ſayd they, is the Terror of the French, / The Scar-Crovv that affrights our Children ſo.}}
#* '''22 March 2012''', Scott Tobias, AV Club ''The Hunger Games''[https://web.archive.org/web/20120324175255/http://www.avclub.com/articles/the-hunger-games,71293/]
#*: In movie terms, it suggests Paul Verhoeven in Robocop/Starship Troopers mode, an R-rated bloodbath where the grim '''spectacle''' of children murdering each other on television is bread-and-circuses for the age of reality TV, enforced by a totalitarian regime to keep the masses at bay.
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} Drama and excitement in a display, product, performance, exhibition, etc.
#: {{ux|en|I enjoyed her presentation, but it needs more '''spectacle'''.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} An embarrassing or unedifying scene or situation.
#: {{ux|en|He made a '''spectacle''' out of himself.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} {{attributive form of|en|spectacles}}.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Peleg Parsley Bell|title=Seeing the World. Letter No. 5. {{...|By Peleg Parsley Bell.}} Still in London.|journal=w:The Flag of Our Union|volume=XIII|issue=33|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher=[[w:Maturin Murray Ballou|M{{quote-gloss|aturin}} M{{quote-gloss|urray}} Ballou]]|date=14 August 1858|page=260|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_flag-of-our-union_1858-08-14_13_33/page/260/mode/1up|column=2|passage=Some very interesting relics of Adam and Eve were shown us, that were brought from America; such as a fine-tooth comb, made of thorns, a part of a pair of leather '''spectacle''' frames, without glasses, a boot jack made of ebony, and a set of pawpaws of props, with the backs filled with a hard substance, resembling lava.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|zoology}} The [[brille]] of a [[snake]].
# {{lb|en|countable|rail transport}} A frame with different coloured lenses on a [[semaphore]] [[signal]] through which light from a lamp shines at night, often a part of the signal arm.

